<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsinghua Bamboo Slips</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tsinghua manuscripts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tsinghua Bamboo Slips are a collection of about 2,500 bamboo strips inscribed in the Chu script in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, acquired by Tsinghua University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 2008 after being looted from a tomb. Radiocarbon-dated to about 300 BCE, they include lost chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, a historical chronicle and the Chu ju, an account of the residences of the early Chu rulers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The strips were donated to Tsinghua University in July 2008 by an alumnus, named in the Guangming Daily as Zhao Weiguo, who had bought them at a foreign auction. Where and when they were dug up is unknown, but the illegal excavation was probably of a tomb in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> province. Similarities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the Guodian tomb point to a mid-to-late Warring States tomb in the region culturally dominated by the state of Chu.</p><p>When they reached the university the strips were badly affected by mould. After conservation, a Center for Excavated Texts Research and Preservation was set up at Tsinghua on 25 April 2009, with Li Xueqin directing the project. The team published its first volume of photographs, transcriptions and commentary in 2010, in a series planned at 18 volumes, while studies and translations appear in a series edited by Huang Dekuan and Edward Shaughnessy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Several texts bear on the Shang Shu, the Exalted Writings. The Yin zhi partly overlaps the Tang shi chapter and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of volume nine largely overlaps the Jin teng, while other texts in the same style are missing from the received book, either never taken into the canon or lost in transmission. Volume two holds a single text, the Xinian, on 138 well-preserved strips, probably composed around 370 BCE: it records key events of Zhou history and traces the origin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> to supporters of the Shang dynasty who opposed the Zhou conquest.</p><p>Other texts include a gathering of the Zhou elite in the ancestral temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>, with hymns in the style of the Shi Jing; the Baoxun, presented as King Wen's deathbed admonition to his son on the concept of the Middle; the Xin shi wei zhong, which holds that humans are in charge of their destiny; the Wu yin tu, one of the few writings on music older than the empire; and five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> on judging, treating, training and riding horses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 7 January 2014 the journal Nature announced that 21 of the strips, put in the right order, form a decimal multiplication table able to multiply whole or half numbers up to 99.5. Joseph Dauben of the City University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> called it the earliest artefact of its kind in the world, and Guo Shuchun of the Chinese Society of the History of Mathematics said it filled a gap in the mathematical record before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>. Officials are presumed to have used it to calculate land areas, crop yields and taxes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsinghua Bamboo Slips” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Zhou enfeoffed Yu Xiong's great-grandson Xiong Yi as a viscount at Danyang, in the hills of the Han River region; the location of Danyang is disputed, and the Chu ju, a bamboo text among the <b>Tsinghua University manuscripts</b>, gives a different account of the early rulers' movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
